From: chromeless <platform@triform.dev>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 06:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pulse ADM: reset quit_ in Init() so a re-initialised module
 can record again

AudioDeviceLinuxPulse::Terminate() sets quit_ = true and joins the
record and playout threads. Init() spawns a fresh pair — and never
resets quit_. Both new threads therefore evaluate

    MutexLock lock(&mutex_);
    if (quit_) {
      return false;
    }

on their first wakeup, return false, and exit. The module reports a
successful Init() with two threads that are already gone.

Everything downstream then fails in a way that points somewhere else.
StartRecording() sets _startRec, signals _timeEventRec, and waits on
_recStartEvent for the record thread to connect the stream. Nobody is
listening, so the wait returns without _recording ever being set and
the call takes its second failure path:

    modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc:1091
    RTC_LOG(LS_ERROR) << "failed to activate recording";

That is the SAME string the 10-second timeout path logs, which makes
the two indistinguishable in a log — the give-away is that this one
returns in microseconds rather than after ten seconds.

Why this matters to us
----------------------
Our worker outlives its viewers: the browser process re-arms its
PeerConnection for each new session rather than exiting (see
cloud_browser_browser_main_parts.cc RearmSession). The shared
AudioDeviceModule is Init()'d and Terminate()'d across that boundary,
so the FIRST viewer on a process gets audio and every viewer after it
gets video with silence, for the life of the process — days, in a
standalone deployment.

Measured on 2026-09-10 against a live worker: exactly one
"webrtc_audio_mo" thread alive where Init() spawns two, and
StartRecording returning -1 in microseconds with InitRecording having
just returned 0.

Why not avoid the patch
-----------------------
Four in-tree workarounds were tried and refuted by measurement (see
docs/findings/audio-dies-after-first-rearm.md): explicitly stopping
the ADM before re-arm, a full Terminate/Init cycle, pre-arming the ADM
so AudioState skips its own start, and re-ordering the pre-arm against
the PeerConnection teardown. None can work, because none of them can
reach quit_ — it is private, it is only written by Terminate(), and no
public API clears it.

Upstream considerations
-----------------------
quit_ is a member of the Pulse ADM alone; the ALSA backend in the same
directory has no equivalent latch. Resetting it in Init() restores the
documented contract of Init()/Terminate() as a reusable pair, matches
what a caller reasonably expects from a module that exposes both, and
cannot regress the single-Init case because quit_ is already false
there. Worth sending upstream.

Touched files:
  third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
---
diff --git a/third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc b/third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
--- a/third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
+++ b/third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
@@ -156,6 +156,19 @@ AudioDeviceGeneric::InitStatus AudioDeviceLinuxPulse::Init() {
     return InitStatus::OK;
   }
 
+  // Clear the terminate latch before spawning the threads below.
+  //
+  // Terminate() sets quit_ and joins both threads; without this reset the
+  // fresh pair spawned here evaluate `if (quit_) return false;` on their
+  // first wakeup and exit immediately. Init() then reports OK with no
+  // threads, and StartRecording() waits on _recStartEvent for a record
+  // thread that no longer exists — failing in microseconds with the same
+  // "failed to activate recording" message its 10-second timeout path logs.
+  {
+    MutexLock lock(&mutex_);
+    quit_ = false;
+  }
+
   // Initialize PulseAudio
   if (InitPulseAudio() < 0) {
     RTC_LOG(LS_ERROR) << "failed to initialize PulseAudio";
